  "spans": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "nodeId": "n3",
      "text": "\"Drink at Least 8 Glasses of Water a Day\" - Really?",
      "contextBefore": "* Home * 2002 H2 * 08aug2002 Water For Release: August 8, 2002 Contact: DMS Communications (603) 650-1492 Print Version Article (PDF) ",
      "contextAfter": " *Dartmouth Professor Finds No Scientific Evidence for '8 x 8'* Hanover, NH -- It has become accepted wisdom: \"Drink at least eight glasses of water a day!\" Not necessarily, says DMS physician Heinz V",
      "start": 134,
      "end": 185,
      "extractSha256": "3563c5648f35fb14aca17ad15cb3b0bdc3caa7c154246af809fdc930a9df40b0",
      "windowSha256": "645841caf09a3bace50f3337c481dbc8dbea96cb2379e3b5362a87e7b4ba8d2e",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-18T17:09:14.081Z",
      "waybackUrl": "https://web.archive.org/web/20120405091644/https://geiselmed.dartmouth.edu/news/2002_h2/08aug2002_water.shtml",
      "tier": "exact"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "nodeId": "n4",
      "text": "Despite the seemingly ubiquitous admonition to \"drink at least eight 8-oz glasses of water a day\" (with an accompanying reminder that beverages containing caffeine and alcohol do not count), rigorous proof for this counsel appears to be lacking.",
      "contextBefore": "f physiology. Regulatory, integrative and comparative physiology · 2002-11-01 · 283(5):R993-1004 · DOI: 10.1152/ajpregu.00365.2002 · PMID: 12376390 Publication type: Journal Article, Review Abstract. ",
      "contextAfter": " This review sought to find the origin of this advice (called \"8 x 8\" for short) and to examine the scientific evidence, if any, that might support it. The search included not only electronic modes bu",
      "start": 328,
      "end": 573,
      "extractSha256": "4d0af0069110045105e9b875c53275ac1023640b4a57dca2ee0beff160885159",
      "windowSha256": "a86f16e1b300de945510dbd4108fd65699cd2587d465c2b2e9b6ecd3754f89c0",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-18T17:09:14.727Z",
      "waybackUrl": "https://web.archive.org/web/20200307152749/https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12376390",
      "tier": "exact"
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "nodeId": "n5",
      "text": "A recent editorial (PDF) in the *Journal of the American Society for Nephrology* is getting wide press coverage for debunking the so-called \"8x8\" theory-the popularly held belief that drinking eight 8-ounce glasses of water daily helps remove toxins, improve skin tone, and increase satiety, among other health benefits.",
      "contextBefore": "ink is valid through . There was an error with your gift link. Please try again. Only Slate Plus members can gift Slate stories. Become a member to share 10 free articles a month.Become a Plus Member ",
      "contextAfter": " The authors chalk up the belief to folklore, and newspaper reports claim ignorance as to its provenance. Just how long has this idea been around? Two-hundred years, at least. The most commonly cited ",
      "start": 761,
      "end": 1081,
      "extractSha256": "df4f2356d508651d869a027444688303857de4efcee4afb39e9516af70bc1b15",
      "windowSha256": "4ecabad42f83e0cc0b43969953425a03924477a86bcfdbbd453fe4409194fd70",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-18T17:09:15.139Z",
      "waybackUrl": "https://web.archive.org/web/20190504084330/https://slate.com/news-and-politics/2008/04/who-says-you-need-to-drink-eight-glasses-of-water-a-day.html",
      "tier": "exact"
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "nodeId": "n6",
      "text": "Evidence for drinking 8 glasses of water each day.",
      "contextBefore": ", and it's very easy to remember. But is there truth to this advice, or is it just a myth? This article reviews the evidence behind the \"eight glasses a day\" rule and how much water we need each day. ",
      "contextAfter": " It's hard to pinpoint exactly where and when the \"eight glasses per day\" rule originated. There are theories that it may be based on a fluid intake of 1 ml per calorie of food consumed. For someone e",
      "start": 743,
      "end": 793,
      "extractSha256": "6d0f82e924f351a1a984905183f0fa87036046e82b1131b0633e191139699f3b",
      "windowSha256": "35c3757896f97013dc2a134303f21f64cf63cdf859c086b7a1a5511b0ded3326",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-18T17:09:15.801Z",
      "waybackUrl": "https://web.archive.org/web/20170807094349/https://www.healthline.com/nutrition/8-glasses-of-water-per-day",
      "tier": "exact"
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "nodeId": "n7",
      "text": "Let's look into the details of this eight-glasses-of-water-per-day rule (also known as the \"8x8\" rule because it implies drinking eight glasses of water, each with a volume of 8 oz.)",
      "contextBefore": ".7 liters) for men (1). But this value also includes the water that you ingest with your solid food, which contains from 5 to 90% water, and all the beverages that you drink and not only plain water. ",
      "contextAfter": " In this Article (Index). * Where did the eight-glasses-of-water-a-day rule come from? * How much are eight 8-ounce glasses? * So how much water do you really need? * Water Requirements * Water contai",
      "start": 1706,
      "end": 1888,
      "extractSha256": "29f2baf59148ff89542a543aeba93deeacbb66559c8072c325acb42f0fda81b7",
      "windowSha256": "86da926c91a82ce5cb1ee864bf8758c6a64dfb56018ed977db2a290d8c06caec",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-18T17:09:16.888Z",
      "waybackUrl": "https://web.archive.org/web/20210418073823/https://fit-and-well.com/health/do-i-need-eight-glasses-of-water-a-day.html",
      "tier": "exact"
    }
  ],
